# The fleet root IS a git repository (branch `main`, no remote) tracking the fleet map, the
# orchestration CLI and the generated shell helpers. These entries are what keep the clones,
# the secrets and the CLI's dependencies out of it, so they are load-bearing, not insurance.
# Do not remove them.
#
# `clone_*/` rather than `clone_0*/`: the fleet is no longer fixed at three clones, and the
# old glob would have stopped matching at clone_10.
#
# LEADING SLASHES matter on the root-only entries, and their absence was a real bug: an
# unanchored `clone_*/` matches a directory of that name ANYWHERE in the tree, which silently
# swallowed the whole per-clone half of `app/dev/golden/` -- the regression baseline was
# committed with 15 of its 136 files, and nothing said so. Clones, the secrets file and the two
# shared directories only ever exist at the hangar root, so anchor them there and let a nested
# path of the same name be committable. `node_modules/` stays unanchored deliberately: the CLI's
# own lives at `app/node_modules`.
/.env.shared
.remember/
/clone_*/
node_modules/

# This machine's own hangar config: local paths, ports and token variable names. The committed
# record of it is hangar.config.example.yaml, which is kept a faithful superset. `hangar.schema.json`
# stays TRACKED -- it is generated from the zod schema and both YAML files reference it.
hangar.config.yaml

# The two shared directories the clones cannot share by symlink or by setting:
#   plans/ -- the plan archive. `plansDirectory` must resolve INSIDE the project root (Claude
#             Code follows symlinks and rejects an escape), so each clone keeps its own real
#             .claude/plans and `hangar plans collect` moves finished plans in here.
#   tmp/   -- the shared per-ticket Jira cache and per-clone scratch. Each clone's `tmp` is a
#             symlink to it. Holds Jira attachments and live PID files; never committable.
/plans/
/tmp/

# Generated per-machine state (corepack pnpm shims, Eclipse workspace data). Never committed.
.hangar/

# The colour assignments are INPUT, not cache, and that is why they get their own entry rather
# than sitting under the line above. `hangar colours change <clone> <colour>` writes this file,
# it is the only per-clone value not derived from the index, and NOTHING regenerates it -- so
# `.hangar/` being documented as safe-to-delete generated state does not apply to it. Clearing
# a corepack cache must not take the colours with it.
#
# It was TRACKED, which made it a merge conflict on every `git pull` from a published upstream:
# a tracked file that a hangar command rewrites always is. `colourAssignmentsFile` reads the new
# path and falls back to the old root-level one PERMANENTLY, not just for a migration window --
# a pull into an older checkout restores the tracked file, and the reader still has to find it.
/.hangar/colour-assignments.json

# The two generated shell helpers, for the same reason: `hangar colours sync` rewrites both from
# `app/src/palette.ts`, the hangar id and the clone list, so a published copy is wrong for every
# hangar but the one that committed it. `git rm --cached` left them on disk, so no shell rc that
# sources clone-terminal.sh broke; `add-clone` and `colours sync` recreate them in a fresh hangar.
/clone-colours.sh
/clone-terminal.sh

# The hangar root's own Claude Code settings, generated by `hangar setup` and repaired by
# `hangar doctor --fix`. Two of its three values are ABSOLUTE paths on this machine -- the shared
# memory directory and the mode statusline -- and Claude Code fails SILENTLY on both: no error,
# no log, the badge just never appears. A tracked copy would therefore give a colleague no shared
# memory, no plan archive and no mode badge, with nothing said.
#
# The two `.claude/modes/*.settings.json` files stay TRACKED, and that is deliberate rather than
# an oversight: their permission arrays are operator mode's security boundary, and operator mode
# is denied `Edit(./.claude/modes/**)` while being allowed `Bash(hangar doctor:*)` -- so a
# `--fix` that generated them would let operator mode rewrite its own permission list through a
# command it can run. `doctor` reports their statusline path and offers no repair.
/.claude/settings.json

# The advisory half of the golden capture (`pnpm golden` in app/). Its gated sibling IS tracked
# and is the regression baseline; this half moves with whatever the clones are doing -- a
# branch, a last commit, how many entries the shared tmp/ holds -- so tracking it would dirty
# every `git status` and make the one diff that matters harder to see. A dirty
# app/dev/golden/ therefore always means the GATE moved.
app/dev/golden/advisory/
